  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 643 PM CDT Sat Jun 27 2026\n\nToday-Sunday: Dry Today, Shower and Storm Chances Return Sunday\n\nShortwave ridging and light southeast winds are in place for \ntoday resulting in seasonable temperatures in the upper 70s to \nlow 80s. By Sunday morning a warm front moves northward into our\nCWA and by the mid afternoon will be through the northern \nportions of the forecast area. Ahead of this front, CAMs have \nbeen showing an MCS that moves through Minnesota and into \nWisconsin. The main question is exactly where this MCS tracks. \nThe instability gradient is line up from northwest to southeast \nacross central Minnesota into northeast Iowa and southwest \nWisconsin by the mid morning. If this MCS outruns the gradient \nthen it would more than likely dissipate, but if it can latch \nonto the gradient then it could continue on through the morning.\nWe will have to monitor trends as we get closer to Sunday \nmorning. Outside of this MCS, behind this warm front MUCAPE \nvalues increase into the 2000 to 3000 J/kg range. There are some\nindications that subtle waves may move through during the \nafternoon which could help to initiate some showers and storms. \nThe main uncertainty for storms development is how capped the \nenvironment will be. Despite how much instability and modest \nshear that will be around, if the CAP is too strong then storms \nmay not be able to fire. On the contrary, if the CAP could \nweaken enough via a subtle wave or outflow, then storms would \nhave a better chance of firing. In addition to the storm \npotential, warm and humid conditions begin to move into the \nUpper Midwest as temperatures warm up into the mid to upper 80s \nfor most locations with dewpoints getting into the low to mid \n70s by Sunday afternoon for areas west of the Mississippi River \nand eventually spreading to the rest of the CWA Sunday night \ninto Monday.\n\nMonday-Friday: Hot and Humid Conditions, Periodic Storm Chances\n\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance continues to show the ridge\namplifying off to our east and slightly retrograding through \nthe week. With the Upper Midwest being on the western fringes of\nthe ridge, hot and humid air shifts into our neck of the woods \ndue to strong southwesterly flow in the lower levels. The mean \ntemperatures at 850mb according to the most recent DESI run on \nMonday are between 23 and 25C. Dewpoints increase into the mid \nto upper 70s. As a result, heat indices are forecasted to be in \nthe 100 to 110F range. This has prompted an Extreme Heat Watch \nacross much of the forecast area. Due to the potential for \novernight low temperatures to be in the mid to upper 70s, the \nWatch goes through Tuesday as heat indices are once again in the\n100 to 108F range. This Watch will need to be changed to a Heat\nAdvisory or Extreme Heat Warning as we get closer. While 850mb \ntemperatures, more in the 20 to 22C range, for Wednesday through\nFriday, heat indices range from 95 to 105F. These values would \nrequire a heat headline each day. There is some uncertainty on \nhow hot we get as the flow pattern aloft will favor southwest \nflow, which would allow for waves to move north from the Plains \ninto our region. Both the GEFS and EPS have the majority of \ntheir ensemble members showing measurable precipitation each day\nnext week, with the EPS being a little more excited than the \nGEFS as far as amounts go. These storm chances will play a role \nin how hot we can get by potentially keeping us cooler than \nforecasted, which would mean that a heat headline may not be \nneeded past Tuesday.",
